From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Portal <116828772+Retrorerr@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 18:20:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] backends/anland: Send input before the frame callbacks

Input applied for a select was queued to clients and only flushed when
the event loop next blocked, after the composite the select starts. The
input and that frame's callbacks then reached a client in one write. Qt
dispatches frame callbacks on their own thread, so a Qt Quick client
often handled the callback before the touch: about 8% of its frames
during a drag showed no motion and the next showed twice as much.
Flush client connections as soon as input has been applied.
---
 src/backends/anland/anland_backend.cpp | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/backends/anland/anland_backend.cpp b/src/backends/anland/anland_backend.cpp
--- a/src/backends/anland/anland_backend.cpp
+++ b/src/backends/anland/anland_backend.cpp
@@ -292,12 +292,23 @@
     if (m_inFallback) return;
 
     InputEvent ev;
+    bool applied = false;
     while (poll_input_event(m_display, &ev, 0) > 0) {
         processInputEvent(ev);
+        applied = true;
         if (m_inFallback) break; // poll_input_event may have triggered fallback
     }
     // An event time belongs to the event written with it.
     setAnlandEventTime(std::nullopt);
+
+    // Send the input now instead of when the event loop next blocks. A select
+    // composites right after this, and that frame's callbacks would otherwise
+    // leave in the same write as the input. Qt dispatches frame callbacks on
+    // their own thread, so a Qt Quick client then often handled the callback
+    // before the touch and drew the frame without it.
+    if (applied && !m_inFallback) {
+        waylandServer()->display()->flush();
+    }
 }
 
